1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,705
Greetings, fellow aliens!

2
00:00:02,126 --> 00:00:05,438
You might wonder why I haven't posted any episode for a while.

3
00:00:05,787 --> 00:00:07,725
The answer is simple : Relativity.

4
00:00:08,194 --> 00:00:11,481
I made a trip with 99.5% light speed.

5
00:00:12,018 --> 00:00:15,911
The trip lasted about one moon cycle for me, but ten moon cycles for you.

6
00:00:16,601 --> 00:00:21,984
But now I'm back, with a new episode about a strange and peculiar earthling science : Mathematics.

7
00:00:55,306 --> 00:01:02,192
Earthlings' thirst for knowledge is driven by several factors, like curiosity, doubt, warfare, show-off, and laziness.

8
00:01:02,654 --> 00:01:05,901
The latter, laziness, is what gave rise to mathematics.

9
00:01:06,542 --> 00:01:08,058
We will come back to this later.

10
00:01:08,488 --> 00:01:11,476
In a nutshell, mathematics could be called "science of truth".

11
00:01:11,864 --> 00:01:16,154
To understand this notion, we have to understand the earthling concept of true and false.

12
00:01:16,479 --> 00:01:18,583
In other words: Earthling logic.

13
00:01:19,032 --> 00:01:26,582
If you are an alien, you are probably familiar with the common fuzzy ternary logic: Statements can have the values "True", "false", "Don't know", or anything in-between.

14
00:01:27,407 --> 00:01:30,257
This is often represented as the "uncertainty triangle".

15
00:01:30,883 --> 00:01:34,343
For those unfamiliar with ternary logic, allow me to explain.

16
00:01:34,923 --> 00:01:41,612
Imagine a Centauri lottery: You have a box of six creatures, Tribbles and Snorks, and you draw one of them, hoping to get a Tribble.

17
00:01:42,276 --> 00:01:46,803
Now, the claim, "I'll draw a Tribble", will be true if you know that all the creatures are Tribbles.

18
00:01:47,048 --> 00:01:49,714
If there are only snorks, the claim will be false.

19
00:01:50,178 --> 00:01:55,044
If you have three tribbles and three snorks, the answer is halfway between true and false.

20
00:01:55,348 --> 00:01:58,662
If you have no idea what's in the box, the value is "Don't Know".

21
00:01:59,084 --> 00:02:04,457
When you know that there is one tribble and two snorks but don't know what the rest is, the value will be somewhere within the triangle.

22
00:02:05,002 --> 00:02:11,027
Of course, this value depends on what you know: When you gain information, the value moves away from the "Don't Know" corner.

23
00:02:11,429 --> 00:02:14,709
That is, in a nutshell, the "uncertainty triangle".

24
00:02:15,749 --> 00:02:20,518
Many alien races only know the three corner values and don't care about intermediate values.

25
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:24,733
Political logic is the opposite: The corners of the triangle are cut off.

26
00:02:25,078 --> 00:02:30,474
An alien politician will never clearly say something is true, or false, or admit that he doesn't know.

27
00:02:30,715 --> 00:02:35,795
Well, he might say that he didn't know - this logical value is called "plausible deniability".

28
00:02:36,335 --> 00:02:38,615
Not everything fits into this schema, though.

29
00:02:39,035 --> 00:02:55,342
The administrative logic used internally by the Central Galactic Bureaucracy, for instance, has eleven values: Approved, Rejected, Wrong-form, Bring-More-Documents, File-Lost, Not-This-Desk, This-will-take-some-time, Awaiting-Cash, Nothing-I-can-do, Incorrectly-Stamped and Desk-closed-Come-Back-Later.

30
00:02:57,145 --> 00:03:01,718
The administrative Logic is one of the reasons nobody really knows how the Bureaucracy works.

31
00:03:02,345 --> 00:03:07,445
Space squids, on the other hand, have a unary logic based on one single value, "bloop".

32
00:03:07,765 --> 00:03:11,565
The actual information is transmitted in the nuances on how the "bloop" is pronounced.

33
00:03:12,085 --> 00:03:15,948
It's a bit like the earthling word "awful", which can mean "very good" or "very bad".

34
00:03:16,741 --> 00:03:18,597
Now, Earthling logic is binary.

35
00:03:19,086 --> 00:03:24,486
Earthling brains are composed of cells called neurons which have only two states: Silent and active.

36
00:03:26,086 --> 00:03:29,732
Analogously, earthling logic is based on two values: False and True.

37
00:03:30,295 --> 00:03:31,354
There is no middle ground.

38
00:03:31,894 --> 00:03:38,854
Earthlings believe that all claims have a value "true" or "false" - and when you see neither of them, it's only because the actual value is hidden.

39
00:03:39,318 --> 00:03:41,873
Earthlings call this imaginary hidden value "truth".

40
00:03:42,438 --> 00:03:48,383
By the way, this binary thinking is the reason why earthlings have such problems wrapping their mind around quantum physics.

41
00:03:48,746 --> 00:03:54,966
Their brains can't accept the idea that, for example, two different realities can coexist as long as they are not observed.

42
00:03:55,368 --> 00:03:59,823
If we combine binary logic with the Earthling concept of "things", we get set theory.

43
00:04:00,187 --> 00:04:04,940
A set is a collection of elements, mathematical entities that stand for any kind of "things".

44
00:04:05,466 --> 00:04:09,443
Elements are either in a set or outside: Earthling logic knows no middle ground.

45
00:04:09,932 --> 00:04:15,626
The earthling logic leads what earthling call "conclusions": Rules for combining claims to get new claims.

46
00:04:16,333 --> 00:04:20,333
Conclusions are what the fabric of mathematics is made of - we will come back to that point.

47
00:04:20,693 --> 00:04:25,747
But the roots of mathematics lie in a very practical problem: counting, or more generally measuring.

48
00:04:26,229 --> 00:04:27,029
Let me elaborate.

49
00:04:27,869 --> 00:04:38,329
Every young planetary civilization has to find a way to measure resources: Population, arable land, food, real estate, raw materials, time : All this needs to be properly measured.

50
00:04:38,869 --> 00:04:44,292
Also, construction requires measuring quantities like lengths, areas, volumes and angles beforehand.

51
00:04:45,075 --> 00:04:51,459
Small quantities can be measured directly, but things get more complicated when the quantities are either bigger or not easy to measure.

52
00:04:51,879 --> 00:04:57,279
That's when calculating comes in: It's a shortcut, a way to combine available quantities to get other quantities.

53
00:04:57,879 --> 00:05:00,962
In other words, calculating equals measuring times laziness.

54
00:05:01,361 --> 00:05:04,127
Every alien race has its own calculating methods.

55
00:05:04,392 --> 00:05:08,792
The Aldebaranians, for example, use a curve, to multiply values.

56
00:05:09,032 --> 00:05:11,015
It's a strange method, but it works.

57
00:05:11,436 --> 00:05:15,600
Now, the bigger a civilization becomes, the more things are to calculate.

58
00:05:16,001 --> 00:05:25,534
That's usually the point when a planet's bio-administrator gives the civilization limited access to the Great Galactic Grid, and thus to planetary superbrains which do the calculation work for the whole galaxy.

59
00:05:25,735 --> 00:05:30,262
The access to superbrains is completely free - at least for some hundred solar cycles.

60
00:05:30,262 --> 00:05:39,665
But once the civilization is completely dependent on external computer power, a Tax Star portals into orbit and the planet becomes full tax-paying member of the Galactic Bureaucracy.

61
00:05:40,047 --> 00:05:42,261
At this point, they don't have much of a choice.

62
00:05:42,525 --> 00:05:57,165
The "computation monopoly" of the Planetary Superbrains make them one of the powers that run the Central Galactic Bureaucracy, alongside with the Tax Authority, the Control Bureau, the Apparatus, the Galactic Intelligence, the Space Force, the Committee, the Galactic Bank...

63
00:05:57,385 --> 00:05:58,965
Well, it's complicated.

64
00:05:59,545 --> 00:06:02,661
Actually, nobody really knows who pulls the strings in the Galaxy.

65
00:06:03,565 --> 00:06:10,891
But Mankind had no bio-admin and no access to superbrains - so they had to develop their own way to deal with computations: Mathematics.

66
00:06:11,751 --> 00:06:17,549
Basically, mathematics is a way of avoiding calculating , and calculating is a way of avoiding measuring and counting.

67
00:06:17,970 --> 00:06:22,131
That's why we could define "Mathematics equals measuring times laziness squared".

68
00:06:23,311 --> 00:06:28,871
Tips for tourists: In general, Earthling books are an interesting and exotic souvenir.

69
00:06:29,311 --> 00:06:35,871
However, mathematics is considered contraband by Galactic laws, because it threatens the computation monopoly of the Superbrains.

70
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:41,434
You don't want to run into a hyperspace patrol with a math book on board - it could get you into serious trouble.

71
00:06:42,137 --> 00:06:45,803
The first contact of aliens with mathematics goes back many millennia.

72
00:06:45,944 --> 00:06:57,452
More than four thousand solar cycles ago, a couple of alien information smugglers landed In a region called Egypt, and offered the earthlings advanced computation methods and the exact value of Pi, in exchange for the recipe for beer.

73
00:06:57,914 --> 00:07:09,383
The Egyptians politely declined: Their computation methods were sufficient to build huge pyramids, not to speak of the logistics required to pay and house thousands of workers and to move millions of tons of calcium carbonate.

74
00:07:09,544 --> 00:07:16,526
As for Pi, they had already incorporated Pi into the dimensions of some of their pyramids, using rolled and stacked cylindrical barrels.

75
00:07:16,807 --> 00:07:22,428
To construct their pyramids, the Egyptians had developed something called Geometry: The theory of shapes.

76
00:07:23,050 --> 00:07:28,890
In a nutshell, geometry is all about lines, angles, and areas, and how to avoid measuring them.

77
00:07:29,230 --> 00:07:34,332
The information smugglers came back a thousand solar cycles later, this time in Babylonia.

78
00:07:34,555 --> 00:07:36,271
But the Babylonians were not impressed.

79
00:07:36,736 --> 00:07:43,724
Their mathematicians knew the Pythagorean theorem, could solve quadratic equations and were able to compute the square root of two, with an error of one ten-thousandth.

80
00:07:45,189 --> 00:07:49,086
More generally, the Babylonians had developed the art of manipulating numbers.

81
00:07:49,508 --> 00:07:51,528
Earthlings call this Arithmetics.

82
00:07:51,873 --> 00:07:52,823
What are numbers ?

83
00:07:53,278 --> 00:07:54,918
Well, it has to do with "things".

84
00:07:55,498 --> 00:08:02,788
To quantify things, earthlings invented numbers, a series of abstract "things", called 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 etc.

85
00:08:03,432 --> 00:08:05,679
Think of numbers as a series of imaginary stickers.

86
00:08:06,153 --> 00:08:10,993
Whenever earthlings want to quantify things, they glue the stickers onto them, one by one.

87
00:08:11,273 --> 00:08:12,835
This is called "counting".

88
00:08:13,379 --> 00:08:16,999
The last assigned sticker indicates the quantity of things, their "number".

89
00:08:17,422 --> 00:08:19,371
Earthlings are obsessed with numbers.

90
00:08:19,639 --> 00:08:26,599
They count and number everything: Houses, offspring, vehicles, books, even kings, and gods - everything.

91
00:08:26,899 --> 00:08:28,979
Except for the microbes.

92
00:08:32,039 --> 00:08:34,169
There are many ways to represent numbers.

93
00:08:34,555 --> 00:08:36,875
But the most common way is the decimal system.

94
00:08:37,415 --> 00:08:43,466
Numbers are written as series of digits - nine of them correspond to the numbers 1 to 9, and an "empty" digit called zero.

95
00:08:44,364 --> 00:08:50,206
The ten digits come from the fact that earthlings have ten fingers - the bony tentacles at the end of their upper extremities.

96
00:08:50,610 --> 00:08:56,515
Some ancient civilizations used a base-12 system or a base-60 system, which are more useful for divisions.

97
00:08:56,975 --> 00:09:00,935
You'll still find remains of both systems, in particular for measuring time.

98
00:09:01,715 --> 00:09:08,987
Earthling computers use a base-2 system, as they are based on true-false logic, not on true-false-don't-know logic like most alien computers.

99
00:09:09,653 --> 00:09:14,633
By the way, Squids have a similar system - but it's a base-one-system which contains only the digit zero.

100
00:09:15,453 --> 00:09:18,822
Any number above zero can't be expressed in this system and is called "some".

101
00:09:19,736 --> 00:09:25,624
The earthlings found numbers quite useful - and when earthlings find something useful, you can bet that they want more of it.

102
00:09:25,947 --> 00:09:30,516
For starters, they took the digit 0 and made it a number, closing the gap below the 1.

103
00:09:31,163 --> 00:09:33,893
Generally, earthling mathematicians don't like gaps.

104
00:09:34,344 --> 00:09:38,244
For example, when you subtract 5 from 2, you fall into a gap "below zero".

105
00:09:39,104 --> 00:09:41,496
So, earthlings invented negative numbers.

106
00:09:42,110 --> 00:09:46,615
Likewise, when you divide 5 by 2, you fall into the gap between 2 and 3.

107
00:09:46,917 --> 00:09:50,829
So the earthlings invented fractions, aka rational numbers.

108
00:09:50,829 --> 00:09:53,967
Those numbers can be represented as a nice straight line.

109
00:09:54,190 --> 00:10:01,124
In ancient Egypt and Babylon, mathematics was just a set of practical rules, a toolbox for architects and bookkeepers.

110
00:10:01,647 --> 00:10:03,348
But this approach was about to change.

111
00:10:03,834 --> 00:10:10,494
About 2300 solar cycles ago, our alien information smugglers came back a third time, again in Egypt.

112
00:10:11,014 --> 00:10:16,753
Here they met a greek mathematician named Euclid who had developed a wholly new approach to mathematics: Axioms.

113
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:21,586
Realizing they had nothing to top that, our aliens left, never to return again.

114
00:10:22,733 --> 00:10:24,373
But what are those axioms?

115
00:10:24,833 --> 00:10:28,375
Well, axioms are fundamental assumptions you build a theory upon.

116
00:10:28,746 --> 00:10:33,272
Take, for example, the natural numbers 1 2 3 4 5 etc.

117
00:10:33,737 --> 00:10:37,027
Those numbers can be described with the so-called Peano axioms.

118
00:10:37,372 --> 00:10:38,556
There is a number called zero.

119
00:10:39,372 --> 00:10:40,692
Every number has a successor.

120
00:10:41,592 --> 00:10:42,982
Zero is successor of nobody.

121
00:10:43,305 --> 00:10:45,567
No two numbers have the same successor.

122
00:10:45,944 --> 00:10:51,710
If a property holds for 0, and it propagates from each number to its successor, then it holds for all numbers.

123
00:10:52,051 --> 00:10:53,291
This is called "induction".

124
00:10:53,771 --> 00:10:58,371
Given any set of axioms, you can use logic to construct a whole building of conclusions.

125
00:10:59,057 --> 00:11:01,334
Interesting conclusions are called "theorems".

126
00:11:01,761 --> 00:11:04,454
That's basically how the whole of modern mathematics is built.

127
00:11:05,237 --> 00:11:08,425
Note that mathematicians don't think axioms are based on "truth".

128
00:11:08,711 --> 00:11:10,751
They are just assumptions to start with.

129
00:11:11,431 --> 00:11:16,274
Mathematicians like to play with axioms, omit this one or add that one, and see what happens.

130
00:11:16,776 --> 00:11:22,034
Note that mathematics never says "That is true", but rather "Given this and this, you can conclude that".

131
00:11:22,499 --> 00:11:27,415
In other words, mathematical theorems are always true, because they are elaborate tautologies.

132
00:11:28,004 --> 00:11:31,137
One could define mathematics as "hunt for interesting tautologies".

133
00:11:31,444 --> 00:11:37,644
This is the theoretical side of mathematics - there is still the practical one: Measuring times laziness squared.

134
00:11:38,264 --> 00:11:45,588
Now, according to mathematicians, doing mathematics is not so much constructing into the sky as it is exploring an unknown territory.

135
00:11:45,989 --> 00:11:49,537
You have to construct the roads and bridges, but the landscape is already there, waiting.

136
00:11:49,924 --> 00:11:53,564
So, let's explore a bit the landscape of mathematics, shall we?

137
00:11:54,104 --> 00:11:58,562
At the very center we have basic logic, and the two disciplines Geometry and Arithmetics.

138
00:11:59,068 --> 00:12:04,911
As said before, Geometry is all about shapes and measures, and arithmetics is all about numbers and operations.

139
00:12:05,313 --> 00:12:08,013
This is the starting point for the exploration of mathematics.

140
00:12:08,953 --> 00:12:21,106
Starting from here, we will discover several regions of mathematics: Number theory and algebra, which are derived from arithmetics, Topology, which comes from geometry, and Analysis, which is arithmetics used to measure geometry.

141
00:12:21,787 --> 00:12:23,416
Let's start with topology.

142
00:12:23,828 --> 00:12:27,066
Topology is, so to speak, geometry minus measuring.

143
00:12:27,329 --> 00:12:32,244
It's kind of "rubber geometry", it considers properties of shapes that don't change when you deform them.

144
00:12:32,608 --> 00:12:36,363
If you explore the properties of natural numbers, you get to number theory.

145
00:12:36,771 --> 00:12:42,131
Number theorists are particularly fond of prime numbers - numbers that can't be broken down into smaller factors.

146
00:12:42,791 --> 00:12:47,347
Now, arithmetics can be further generalized, with a little trick mathematicians are fond of.

147
00:12:47,750 --> 00:12:51,367
First, you take an example - say, the natural numbers.

148
00:12:51,811 --> 00:12:53,989
Secondly, you state some basic rules.

149
00:12:54,523 --> 00:12:58,659
And third, you forget about the example and consider those rules as axioms.

150
00:12:58,984 --> 00:13:02,104
This is exactly how arithmetics is generalized into Algebra.

151
00:13:02,813 --> 00:13:12,553
For example, "Adding", can not only be applied to numbers, but also to other things like arrows, spatial movements, permutations, rotations, or even dancing steps.

152
00:13:13,213 --> 00:13:16,861
Roughly speaking: When you generalize the rules for addition , you get semi-groups.

153
00:13:17,779 --> 00:13:20,252
When you include subtraction, you get groups.

154
00:13:20,515 --> 00:13:22,825
When you allow multiplication, you get rings.

155
00:13:23,230 --> 00:13:25,970
And when you make sure you can divide, you get fields.

156
00:13:26,770 --> 00:13:28,822
This belongs all to the domain of Algebra.

157
00:13:29,264 --> 00:13:32,544
In a certain manner, algebra is all about manipulating symbols.

158
00:13:34,744 --> 00:13:35,684
Scientific advice.

159
00:13:36,109 --> 00:13:41,341
If you are a Xeno-biologist, you probably know Bloblings - jellyfish-like creatures of various shapes.

160
00:13:42,006 --> 00:13:47,383
Now, the classification of bloblings is basically a problem of topology - but you can do it using group theory.

161
00:13:47,706 --> 00:13:50,137
In other words, adding and subtracting.

162
00:13:50,699 --> 00:13:56,592
See, bloblings are into what earthlings would call bondage - they like to wrap closed rubber bands around their body.

163
00:13:56,936 --> 00:14:06,516
There are countless bondage methods, but you can define "addition" and "subtraction" between bondage loops: This loop plus that loop is that loop, or that loop minus twice this loop is that loop.

164
00:14:07,176 --> 00:14:14,320
Now, for this particular blobling, all bondage variants can be obtained by combining two loops : Through the hole, and around the hole.

165
00:14:15,020 --> 00:14:17,182
Mathematicians would say it is a group of rank two.

166
00:14:17,481 --> 00:14:20,885
Other bloblings need four loops, or six, or fourteen.

167
00:14:21,231 --> 00:14:25,971
This number doesn't change when we deform the blobling - that's something topologists really like.

168
00:14:26,211 --> 00:14:29,251
So, this number can be used to classify the bloblings.

169
00:14:30,351 --> 00:14:37,354
If this was a bit too much for you, don't mind - the important thing here is that we can use algebra to solve problems in topology.

170
00:14:37,574 --> 00:14:41,254
This domain halfway between topology and algebra is called algebraic TOPOLOGY.

171
00:14:42,534 --> 00:14:50,614
Algebra is a bit like what earthlings call magic: You manipulate some symbols and apply some formulae - and suddenly you have performed something completely unrelated.

172
00:14:51,714 --> 00:14:55,823
One could say that any sufficiently evolved algebra is indistinguishable from magic.

173
00:14:56,289 --> 00:14:59,490
This holds even more for another discipline: Category theory.

174
00:14:59,890 --> 00:15:02,488
This theory is all about stuff connected with arrows.

175
00:15:03,011 --> 00:15:05,393
It looks like fiddle-faddle but is surprisingly useful.

176
00:15:06,044 --> 00:15:09,873
Mathematicians call the use of category theory in other disciplines "abstract nonsense".

177
00:15:10,443 --> 00:15:12,623
I'm not joking, it's called like that.

178
00:15:13,083 --> 00:15:14,066
"Abstract nonsense".

179
00:15:14,823 --> 00:15:23,903
Now, we have seen two big regions of mathematics: Topology, which is basically geometry minus measuring, and algebra, which is arithmetics minus numbers.

180
00:15:24,343 --> 00:15:28,816
A third big field of mathematics takes the numbers from arithmetics and uses them to measure geometry.

181
00:15:29,038 --> 00:15:30,418
This is called Analysis.

182
00:15:30,918 --> 00:15:35,105
To understand analysis, we first have to understand something else: Infinity.

183
00:15:35,729 --> 00:15:36,698
What's infinity?

184
00:15:37,132 --> 00:15:41,908
Well, it's another gap-filler: When you count and count onwards, the numbers actually go nowhere.

185
00:15:42,334 --> 00:15:46,252
So mathematicians invented a number "behind" all natural numbers: Infinity.

186
00:15:46,881 --> 00:15:53,124
Once you have the concept of infinity, you can observe, for example, infinitely long increasing sequences of numbers.

187
00:15:53,544 --> 00:16:00,913
Some tend to infinity, others approach some rational number - but there are others that approach nothing, that fall into a gap.

188
00:16:01,134 --> 00:16:03,177
But mathematicians don't like gaps.

189
00:16:03,432 --> 00:16:07,196
So they defined a new kind of numbers to fill the gaps: Real numbers.

190
00:16:07,601 --> 00:16:11,165
A real number is anything that can be approached by an infinite sequence of numbers.

191
00:16:11,796 --> 00:16:18,586
Now, real numbers are very useful to measure things - lengths, angles, areas, time and so on.

192
00:16:18,966 --> 00:16:23,188
Analysis is particularly interested in "functions" - measures that change over time.

193
00:16:23,529 --> 00:16:29,853
This leads to two things: Accumulated values, or "integrals", and change rates, or "differentials".

194
00:16:30,235 --> 00:16:32,815
Turns out that the one is actually the inverse of the other.

195
00:16:33,135 --> 00:16:35,032
That's the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.

196
00:16:35,547 --> 00:16:40,715
Analysis has many practical applications in engineering and natural science, particularly physics.

197
00:16:41,239 --> 00:16:43,979
We will speak about earthling sciences in another episode.

198
00:16:46,439 --> 00:16:54,710
Strategic advice: When attacking a planet, a common tactic is to cut off the defender from the Great Galactic Grid, to block all ballistic computations.

199
00:16:55,071 --> 00:16:59,411
However, earthlings' artillery doesn't need to access the Grid, they use mathematics.

200
00:17:00,031 --> 00:17:02,931
So you better anticipate precisely aimed artillery fire.

201
00:17:05,131 --> 00:17:08,509
With real numbers, mathematicians have filled most of the gaps.

202
00:17:08,777 --> 00:17:13,784
But one gap remains: There is still no square root of minus one, or any other negative number.

203
00:17:14,144 --> 00:17:17,968
So mathematicians invented a square root of minus one and called it "i".

204
00:17:18,308 --> 00:17:21,548
They didn't quite know where to put it, so they put it above the line.

205
00:17:21,808 --> 00:17:27,266
This new number, and everything you can create by combining real numbers with i, are the complex numbers.

206
00:17:27,447 --> 00:17:29,319
Back to our mathematical landscape.

207
00:17:29,710 --> 00:17:34,820
Like between algebra and topology, there are interdisciplinary fields between analysis and the other fields.

208
00:17:35,042 --> 00:17:41,483
When you combine analysis with topology, you get differential geometry - the theory of shapes with notions of length and angles.

209
00:17:42,005 --> 00:17:45,881
It's very useful, for example for speculating about the shape of the universe.

210
00:17:46,283 --> 00:17:52,385
On the border between analysis and algebra we have algebraic geometry - the theory of shapes defined by polynomials.

211
00:17:52,827 --> 00:17:57,307
One example would be this heart-shaped surface, which is defined by this equation.

212
00:17:57,907 --> 00:18:02,649
Earthling viewers, feel free to forward the formula to mathematically inclined reproduction partners.

213
00:18:03,178 --> 00:18:05,678
I understand earthlings like to do such things.

214
00:18:05,986 --> 00:18:06,984
I don't know why.

215
00:18:07,208 --> 00:18:08,428
It's probably a ritual.

216
00:18:09,688 --> 00:18:13,540
There are many other mathematical disciplines, for example probability theory.

217
00:18:13,805 --> 00:18:19,011
It's basically an extension of binary logic to the whole true-false-edge of the uncertainty triangle.

218
00:18:19,554 --> 00:18:23,549
Earthlings think that even uncertain claims are somewhere between true and false.

219
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:27,140
There is still a whole dimension missing, but it's already an improvement.

220
00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:31,170
I will conclude with a theorem that doesn't belong to the fields mentioned above.

221
00:18:31,617 --> 00:18:34,155
It's rather out in the ocean of Logic and meta-mathematics.

222
00:18:34,728 --> 00:18:36,704
I'm speaking of Gödel's first theorem.

223
00:18:36,974 --> 00:18:40,090
It's closely related to Earthlings' "true or false" logic.

224
00:18:40,457 --> 00:18:45,430
See, most mathematical claims in a theory can either be proven true, or proven false.

225
00:18:45,834 --> 00:18:48,944
But Gödel constructed a claim that says "I can't be proven".

226
00:18:49,374 --> 00:18:54,196
The claim can't be false - otherwise it would be a false provable claim; and that would be nonsense.

227
00:18:54,804 --> 00:18:57,853
So, according to earthling logic, it must be true.

228
00:18:58,094 --> 00:19:00,785
But if it's true, it is right about being unprovable.

229
00:19:01,093 --> 00:19:04,346
So we have a claim that is true, but you can't prove it within the theory.

230
00:19:04,784 --> 00:19:11,760
In other words, mathematics can prove a lot, but it will never be able to prove or disprove everything, there will always be open questions.

231
00:19:12,284 --> 00:19:14,883
This was the thirteenth episode of Earthlings 101.

232
00:19:15,456 --> 00:19:20,051
The next episode will be about the two thinking systems Earthlings have : Intuition and reason.

233
00:19:20,254 --> 00:19:25,621
But before that, I'll have to make the third and last episode of my "No Edge" series about the shape of the universe.

234
00:19:26,124 --> 00:19:27,724
Subscribe if you don't want to miss it.

235
00:19:28,144 --> 00:19:31,806
Thanks for watching, and as always: Don't forget to be alien.

